[Pedagogy audit v2 · New teaching] Students are asked to find unknown sides from Question 5 onward, but nothing in the booklet shows how to match corresponding sides and set up the proportion. A short worked example after the identify question is needed.
Worked example: The two triangles are similar. Find the value of $x$.

[tikz]
% mathsmap-inline-width-mm 60
% mathsmap-diagram-colours {"version":1,"kind":"geometry","base":[],"semantic":[],"reference":"j4-worked-diagram"}
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=9mm,y=9mm,line width=0.8pt,every node/.style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont,text=black}]
\special{dvisvgm:raw <metadata data-graph-strokes="1"/>}
\coordinate (A) at (0,0);\coordinate (C) at (2.4,0);\coordinate (B) at (0.825,0.871);
\coordinate (P) at (3.2,0);\coordinate (R) at (6.4,0);\coordinate (Q) at (4.3,1.161);
\draw[black] (A)--(B)--(C)--cycle;\draw[black] (P)--(Q)--(R)--cycle;
\fill[black] (0.275,0.119) circle[radius=0.6mm];\fill[black] (3.567,0.159) circle[radius=0.6mm];
\draw[black,line width=0.4pt] (0.879,0.525) circle[radius=0.6mm];\draw[black,line width=0.4pt] (4.372,0.7) circle[radius=0.6mm];
\node[below left] at (A) {\$A\$};\node[above] at (B) {\$B\$};\node[below right] at (C) {\$C\$};
\node[below left] at (P) {\$P\$};\node[above] at (Q) {\$Q\$};\node[below right] at (R) {\$R\$};
\path (A)--(B) node[midway,above left] {\$6\,\mathrm{cm}\$};
\path (A)--(C) node[midway,below] {\$12\,\mathrm{cm}\$};
\path (P)--(Q) node[midway,above left] {\$8\,\mathrm{cm}\$};
\path (P)--(R) node[midway,below] {\$x\,\mathrm{cm}\$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
The dot and circle angles are equal, so $PQ$ corresponds to $AB$ (each joins a dot angle to a circle angle), and $PR$ corresponds to $AC$.
Write the ratio of corresponding sides with the unknown on top, then solve.
$$\begin{align*}\frac{PR}{AC}&=\frac{PQ}{AB}\\\frac{x}{12}&=\frac{8}{6}\\x&=12\times\frac{8}{6}\\x&=16\text{ cm}\end{align*}$$
Guided practice:
Proposed question: For this pair of similar triangles:
a) find the value of $x$
(with a diagram)
Answers: a) $x=8\text{ cm}$
